  "description": "A quick win: understand The Startle Reflex and Early Reflexes.",
  "is_free": false,
  "published": true,
  "story": {
    "hook": {
      "text": "A loud noise rings out and a newborn flings both arms wide, arches the back, and cries. It was hardwired before birth.",
      "visual": "😲"
    },
    "buildup": {
      "text": "The Moro reflex, along with rooting, sucking, and grasping, is driven by the brainstem, the most mature brain region at birth.",
      "visual": "🧠"
    },
    "discovery": {
      "text": "These primitive reflexes exist because the cortex is not yet in control.",
      "visual": "🤖"
    },
    "twist": {
      "text": "As the cortex matures over the first year, it gradually inhibits these reflexes.",
      "visual": "🔍"
    },
    "climax": {
      "text": "Pediatricians test these reflexes at checkups precisely because their presence and disappearance map the timeline of cortical.",
      "visual": "👨‍⚕️"
    },
    "punchline": {
      "text": "Infant reflexes are not quirks.",
      "visual": "📋"
    }
  },
  "quiz": {
    "question": "Why do primitive reflexes disappear during the first year of life?",
    "options": [
      "The maturing cortex gradually inhibits brainstem-driven responses",
      "The muscles become too strong to produce them",
      "The infant consciously decides to stop performing them"
    ],
    "correct": 0
  },
